// expect: answers f = 42
// why: a lambda's parameter captured by an inner lambda needs a cell, keyed by the lambda that owns it
// from: issues/lang/0296c

/**
 * The inner lambda reads `a`, which is the **outer lambda's parameter**.
 *
 * A captured local lives in a one-field cell so the lambda and the code it came from share one
 * storage, and the emitter records "this needs a cell" against the position of whatever owns it.
 * That record was keyed by `walkFuncLine`/`walkFuncCol`, which name the innermost *declaration* —
 * a function or a method, never a lambda. So `a`'s cell was recorded against the enclosing
 * function, which makes no cell, while the inner lambda's environment was built expecting one:
 * `struct.new` was handed a raw value where a `(ref null …)` belonged and the engine refused the
 * module.
 *
 * Three lambdas nested capturing a *function* local were always fine, and so was one level of it.
 * It takes a parameter of a lambda, read from inside another lambda, and that is what this is.
 *
 * Written without capabilities because the corpus grants none — the shape is the same one
 * `p.then(a => { q.then(b => … a …); })` has, which is how two dependent asynchronous steps are
 * ordinarily written and is why every continuation-passing program met it.
 */
export i32 f() {
  i32[] n = i32[1]();
  fn[void(i32)] outer = (i32 a) => {
    fn[void()] inner = () => { n[0] = a + 1; };
    inner();
  };
  outer(41);
  return n[0];
}
